abstract: >-
  A research apparatus for Ignatian spirituality, confined to the old Society
  (1540-1773) and organised in five lines: the founder's own writings (the
  Exercises in a trilingual public-domain parallel edition, the letters of
  1524-1547 in full), with the founding bull Regimini militantis Ecclesiae
  of 1540 carried complete, bilingual, with the Formula of the Institute -
  the corpus opens and closes with the two papal documents that made and
  unmade the old Society; the school of discernment, carried to its end - the
  Official Directory of 1599 (bilingual), Favre's Memoriale (bilingual),
  Nadal's In Examen annotationes, Rodriguez's Of Conformity to the Will of
  God complete in all thirty-four chapters, the Abandonment to Divine
  Providence attributed to Caussade, carried whole in Ramiere's arrangement
  of 1861 with the attribution stated as debated, and, beneath the school,
  its doctrine of the soul - the Ratio Studiorum of 1599 in its rules for
  the professors of theology and philosophy, the Prooemium of the Coimbra
  De anima commentary, and three chapters of Suarez's De anima commentary
  (all bilingual); the Society in the
  world, likewise carried to its end - Xavier's letters, Acosta's Natural
  and Moral History in Grimston's English of 1604, Ricci and Trigault's
  China in Purchas's digest of 1625, the Jesuit Relations from New France
  in Thwaites's translation with his page numbers on every paragraph, and
  the Imago primi saeculi with its Dutch mirror, emblem by emblem;
  the conscience of the Society - Friedrich Spee (the Cautio Criminalis
  and the Trutznachtigall), and the limpieza de sangre dossier: the
  decree De genere of 1593 with the three recorded intercessions,
  Ribadeneira's memorial against it complete in all thirteen razones
  (with his eyewitness testimony of Ignatius calling Jewish lineage a
  grace), and the mitigation of 1608, bilingual; and Molina's
  examination of the slave trade (De iustitia et iure, disp. 34-35,
  eye-transcribed from the Venice printing of 1594, bilingual - the
  merchants questioned, the verdict of mortal sin, the evangelization
  argument refused - with the Society's own slaveholding stated as
  failure beside the voice); and the
  counter-voices, from the Monita secreta and
  Pascal to the brief of 1773 carried complete. The first program is
  editorially complete; a second, cultural program - the Reductions of
  Paraguay, Kircher's Musurgia, Pozzo's Perspectiva, the Jesuit stage -
  has opened, and its three named modules have shipped: the Reductions
  of Paraguay - five chapters of Muratori's Relation in the London
  English of 1759, with four bilingual passages of Montoya's Conquista
  espiritual; Kircher's Musurgia universalis of 1650 - the Synopsis of
  the ten books, the voices of the birds with Iconismus III carried as
  a page image, and the doctrine of the affects of Book VII, bilingual;
  Pozzo's Perspectiva in the bilingual London printing of 1707 -
  the preface that draws every line to the glory of God, the first
  lesson of the method, and the Sant'Ignazio dossier with the plates of
  the Holy Week apparatus and the feigned cupola, approved by Wren,
  Vanbrugh and Hawksmoor. A sixth reading path, the Society as culture,
  binds the second program from the Ratio's classrooms to the court of
  China; with Bidermann's Cenodoxus, carried from the Munich Ludi
  theatrales of 1666 after its public-domain condition was discharged,
  the cultural program is complete. With a canonical
  paragraph-level citation index, a cross-corpus concordance over the whole
  apparatus, a concept atlas, curated reading paths, a discernment lexicon,
  registers of persons and places, and a citation-bound dialogue with the
  corpus. Four modern translations remain in copyright: their structure and
  anchors are part of the apparatus, but no text of them is distributed - a
  reader opens them from a copy they own.
